en:
What time is it now...?
I can't see a clock anywhere.
Well, of course not.{p}

This old school building is going to be torn down soon, after all.
...Why am I here?
Right. Hino, my senior in the club, told me to put together a feature on the school's seven wonders.{p}

And seven people were supposed to gather... but only six came.
And then I...
Why am I in the old school building now?{p}

Where is he planning to go?
If I keep following him, will we reach wherever he's headed?
He'd been chatting so cheerfully and warmly, but ever since we entered the old school building he hasn't said a word. He just walks on in silence.{p}

...Here we are, the old school building.
Everyone, are you ready?
We're going to a certain classroom in here.{p}

I thought so.
Unless you're really smart, everybody fails once or twice.
Me, I'm a repeat offender, ha ha ha...{p}

There were six students kept behind that time.
Just like us now.
Four boys and two girls.{p}

In this classroom with no air conditioning, in the old school building gone quiet after school, they wiped away their sweat as they worked on their make-up class assignments.
The teacher only came by now and then to check on them.
But then something urgent came up, so the teacher asked another teacher to take over and went home.{p}

I'm sorry.
I'm not saying this to be mean, you know.
I'm saying it for your own good.{p}

That's the clock, right there.
Look...
The clock hanging on that wall.{p}

I don't know why, or whether someone is moving them, but this clock always ends up stuck at 10:20.
Even with the battery out.
...Now, I'll go on with the story.{p}

The six left behind decided to examine the clock too.
They figured the clock had to be broken after all.
And when one of them took the clock down off the wall...{p}

"AAAAAAAAAAAAGH!"
That was when it happened.
An anguished scream broke the silence.{p}

First, he tried looking for the restroom on the first floor.
Naturally, anyone would go to the restroom closest to this classroom.
But even from a distance, he could see the restroom lights weren't on.{p}

So he went closer, and what do you know, he heard voices talking somewhere up the stairs.
They were low mumbles, hard to make out, but they were definitely people's voices.
And so he climbed the stairs.{p}

Just to be sure, he peeked into the second-floor restroom too, but the lights weren't on there either.
They had to be on the third floor.
Those two were up on the third floor, talking about something.{p}

And if they were talking, it was obvious what about.
They were plotting how to scare the friend who'd come to rescue them.
The thought made him a little angry.{p}

Slowly, slowly, he climbed the stairs.
The voices were coming from the top of the stairs.
No matter how quietly you try to walk, you can't silence the sound of climbing stairs.{p}

Every time the soles of his feet pressed down on a step, the wood bowed and sent a heavy creak echoing all around.
So instead, he focused all his attention on his ears.
He was trying to pin down where they were.{p}

Three times. Knock... knock... knock... like that.
But there was nobody in there, remember?
And then you sense a strange presence, and when you happen to look up...{p}

...This door, huh?
He put his hand on the knob and slowly opened the door.
It wasn't locked.{p}

...Next, it's this door, huh?
He put his hand on the knob and slowly opened the door.
It wasn't locked.{p}

...There was nobody inside.
Now there were only two left.
Which one do you think he opened?{p}

...Right, this door.
He put his hand on the knob and slowly opened the door.
It wasn't locked.{p}

Somewhere, he heard a scream.
Without thinking, he let go of the doorknob.
And he ran toward the voice.{p}

The trail led up to the third floor.
He followed it frantically.
By the time he reached the third floor, they were already out of sight.{p}

But there was a trail of blood on the hallway floor, you see.
He followed that trail.
Where do you think the trail of blood led?{p}

I had to go.
For some reason, I was burning with a sense of mission.
That girls' restroom was definitely calling to me.{p}

"It'll be fine."
That's what I said, but there was no guarantee of anything.
How unconvincing my words sounded.{p}

What was it?
I followed his gaze.
He was looking behind me.{p}

In that instant, terror hit me like ice water poured down my back.
Someone was behind me.
I slowly turned around.{p}

In no time at all, Hosoda was gone from sight, and there was nothing left but an ordinary ceiling.
Even if I told people this ceiling had swallowed Hosoda, no one would believe me.
No, more to the point, I didn't even know whether I'd make it home alive.{p}

He headed straight for the old door to leave.
{f:I|I} panicked.
Just as I was about to call him back, Hosoda suddenly turned around.{p}

With those parting words, the door slammed shut.
{f:I|I} was left behind, all alone.
Had he not noticed something was wrong with {f:me|me}{f:?|?}
Did he think I was just some cheeky underclassman sulking and refusing to answer?
I got mad at Hosoda for being so oblivious.
But I had bigger things to worry about right now{f:.|.}
{f:I|I} took a deep breath.
I had to calm down.
A lot had happened today{f:.|.}
Maybe all that tension had brought on an attack of paralysis.{p}

I'd read about it somewhere{f:.|.}
That more than fifty percent of paralysis attacks in teenagers have nothing to do with ghosts or anything.
That it usually happens when your body is tired or you're under stress.
That had to be {f:it|it}.
It had to be{f:.|.}
{f:I|I} took another deep breath.
That was when it happened.{p}

I heard footsteps: tap... tap...
They were coming this way.{p}Who {f:was it|was it}?
Had Hosoda come back for me{f:?|?}

Who {f:could it be|could it be}?
As I wondered, {f:I|I} realized something important.
The floors of the old school building were half-rotten wood{f:.|, weren't they?}
Footsteps couldn't possibly ring out like that{f:.|.}
But the footsteps kept getting louder: clack... clack...
Were they trying to come in here{f:?|?}{p}

{f:I|I} squeezed my eyes shut.
Was this {f:the end|the end}★
......But the door didn't open.{p}

Something slimy, like firm jelly, slid across my tongue.
A raw, fishy stench.
I couldn't help gagging.{p}

...I see. That's good.
Sorry for saying such strange things.
I'm only saying it for your own good, you know.{p}

I found the restroom right away.
I hurried inside.
And there, {f:I|I} saw something unbelievable.{p}

...For an instant, I thought the stains moved.
Startled, I brought my face even closer.
"...What are you doing?"{p}

It came from the other side of the door.
A teacher?
Thinking so, they hurried back to their seats, but nobody came in.{p}

When they turned around, there stood a woman with a bobbed haircut.
Was the laughter hers?
They couldn't tell.{p}

Dropping his head onto the floor, the woman came into the restroom.
They were next!
They knew it, but their bodies wouldn't move.{p}

As if to cut off Hosoda's words, there was a loud clunk.
For a moment, {f:we|we} froze.
It came from the other side of the door{f:|}.
{f:Just like in Hosoda's story.|Just like in Hosoda's story...}
Timidly, I opened the door.{p}

Just as I looked up, a shadow suddenly fell over me.
Someone's arm wrapped around {f:my|my} neck.
In pain and terror, {f:I|I} desperately twisted my head to see who it was.{p}

I see.
Then I'll show it to you.
Hosoda reached up and took the clock down off the wall.{p}

Just then, something came out from behind the clock and slipped swiftly away along the wall... or so it seemed.
Was it just my imagination{f:|}?
Hosoda didn't say anything, either.{p}

I don't know why, or whether someone is moving them, but this clock always ends up stuck at 10:20.
Even with the battery out.
...Now, I'll go on with the story.{p}

The six left behind decided to examine the clock too.
They figured the clock had to be broken after all.
And when one of them took the clock down off the wall...{p}

Right.
They decided to search the classroom too.
There was no point in walking down that hallway forever.{p}

"Aaaaaaaaaagh!"
They heard a scream that seemed to ring through the whole building.
And at the same time, the sound of something snapping and cracking.{p}

The next instant, she was dragged into the darkness.
It was as if something lurking in the dark had yanked her in.
Crack, crunch... A dull, sickening sound.{p}

Realizing that, he dragged the girl back and shut the door.
As if that would somehow keep the darkness out.
But that was when he saw it.{p}

The next instant, she was swallowed by the darkness.
With a loud crunching, splintering sound.
He didn't let that opening slip by.{p}

He took off running in the opposite direction from the darkness.
He meant to get away from that thing and escape any way he could, even out a window.
Luckily, this part of the hallway wasn't twisted.{p}

When he woke up, he was lying in a hallway of the old school building.
Why had he survived?
Wondering that, he walked out of the building.{p}

Over and over again, until the monster fell and stopped moving.
Apparently he felt really triumphant.
Then, out of nowhere, he heard someone's voice.{p}

There was a crack in the wall in front of them.
And it wasn't just any crack in a wall.
Something like a thin mist was seeping in from the other side of it.{p}

"{f:I'm|I'm} going back to the club room."
I meant it.
I opened the door and stepped out into the hallway.{p}

Hosoda seemed to be saying something behind me{f:, but|, but} {f:I didn't care|I ignored him}.
I walked to the end of the hallway and turned the corner.
...A long hallway stretched on before me.{p}

When he got there, light was spilling out of the restroom.
So it was here after all.
He peered into the restroom.{p}

One after another they dropped to the floor and huddled together in a tumbling, rolling heap.
Then they slipped right past him as he stood frozen, and rolled out of the restroom.
They didn't seem to see him at all.{p}

I'm so happy.
We really are best friends.
Come on, then, let's go.{p}

As he said that, Hosoda pulled a knife out of his pocket.
The blade sprang out with a snap.
Small, but it had a wicked, sharp gleam.{p}

Swish. A hot line ran across my belly.
A moment later came pain so bad I couldn't breathe.
A feeling like my skin was being stretched.{p}

I'd had all I could take.
"Let's go back."
With that, I turned on my heel.{p}

In that instant, terror hit me like ice water poured down my back.
Someone was behind me.
I slowly turned around.{p}

But just then, the girl gave a slight shrug.
"Enough."
As she said it, I felt dizzy for a moment.{p}

Leaving those cryptic words behind, the girl vanished into the second stall.
Yes, she literally vanished.
Even when I ran after her and looked in, there wasn't so much as a trace of her left.{p}
